    {
      "id": "be-s9-forecasting-methods-c2",
      "heading": "Three qualitative methods",
      "explanation_md": "| Method | How it works | Strength | Weakness |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Market research or consumer surveys | Surveys and interviews to find customer preferences | Good guide to customer preferences | Hard to develop a good questionnaire |\n| Executive opinion | A group of managers meet and agree a forecast | Good for strategic or new-product forecasting | One person's opinion can dominate |\n| Delphi method | Builds a consensus among a group of experts | Excellent for long-term product demand and technological forecasts | Time consuming to develop |",
      "example_ids": [],
      "key_terms": [
        {
          "term": "Delphi method",
          "meaning": "Builds a consensus among a group of experts; good for long-term and technological forecasts but time consuming",
          "source_ref": "be--session-9#7"
        },
        {
          "term": "Executive opinion",
          "meaning": "A group of managers meet and come up with a forecast",
          "source_ref": "be--session-9#7"
        }
      ],
      "exam_line": "Surveys: customer preferences, hard questionnaire. Executive opinion: strategic, one voice may dominate. Delphi: expert consensus, long-term, slow.",
